{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md | sed -n '212,248p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md | sed -n '730,795p'; sed -n '100,120p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc; sed -n '210,247p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc; rg -n 'pp-str' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc; sed -n '650,670p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md; git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"   212\t  Nothing else is promised about copying: a value appended to a stream is\n   213\t  **not** copied by the stream in general. The ring buffer stores the value\n   214\t  object itself and reads return it (`ringbuffer.cljc:124-131`, V), and the\n   215\t  ABI permits in-instance streams with no crossing and no serialization\n   216\t  (`dao.stream.wasm.md:22-27`). Aliasing across a stream is therefore\n   217\t  possible, and the rule below is what makes it safe.\n   218\t- **Exclusive mutation right and publication (freeze).** A mutable profile\n   219\t  value (bytes, a host array) has exactly one party with the right to\n   220\t  mutate it: the party that allocated it through the profile, until it\n   221\t  **publishes** it. Publication is any of: appending it to a stream (in-memory\n   222\t  or not); placing it inside a value that is appended; handing it to a seam\n   223\t  or a content handle; returning it from a profile entry marked \"fresh\" to a\n   224\t  caller (the caller then holds the right). On publication the value is\n   225\t  **frozen**: no party, the publisher included, may mutate it afterwards, and\n   226\t  a reader may not mutate what it reads. Freezing is transitive: a collection\n   227\t  is frozen with every mutable value nested in it. A party that needs a\n   228\t  mutable copy of a frozen value takes `slice`, `aclone` or `from-vector`.\n   229\t- **Obligations enforceable within H0-H4**: (i) every P entry leaves every\n   230\t  argument byte-identical, asserted by a property test in H1 over the bytes,\n   231\t  text, float and integer namespaces; (ii) every M entry mutates only its\n   232\t  named target and only after prevalidation, asserted in H1 (bytes) and H2\n   233\t  (arrays); (iii) the Jing writer (H4) never mutates its input and returns\n   234\t  fresh bytes, asserted by re-encoding the same input twice and comparing\n   235\t  the input's bytes before and after; (iv) the callers of M entries inside\n   236\t  the adopted closure are enumerated and audited in H2 for the freeze rule:\n   237\t  `dao.data.btree*` (owned node arrays, never appended), `dao.stream.chunks`\n   238\t  (`absorb` into a fresh buffer, `chunks.cljc:27-34`), `dao.jing.cbor`\n   239\t  (`magnitude-bytes` and the byte-string assembly at `:254,1313-1317`, fresh\n   240\t  output), `yin.vm.linker.sign` (`:82`, a fresh seed copy); the list is the\n   241\t  acceptance, and a new caller fails the H0 gate's list 2 until reviewed.\n   242\t  What is **not** enforceable here: a reader mutating a frozen value it\n   243\t  obtained from a stream; that is a composition obligation stated by this\n   244\t  rule and left to review until the deferred conformance suite (§9.4).\n   245\t\n   246\t### 2.5 Borderline cases, decided\n   247\t\n   248\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   730\tarms outside list 1\". List 1 is generated at H0 by the gate from the actual\n   731\tclosure (every closure file holding a non-exempt arm, §3.3) and must equal\n   732\tthe **expected baseline** below, by category, with each file's reason; a\n   733\tdifference either way is an H0 finding to resolve before H1.\n   734\t\n   735\t+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   736\t| Category (reason)          | Files (V, from §1 and the closure)                                                              | After H4                    |\n   737\t+============================+=================================================================================================+=============================+\n   738\t| H1-H4 migration sources    | `yin/vm/integer/host.cljc`, `dao/jing/cbor.cljc`, `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`, `dao/jing.cljc`, | Removed from list 1 as each |\n   739\t|                            | `yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc`, `yin/vm/debruijn.cljc`, `yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc`,                 | slice lands; `sign.cljc`    |\n   740\t|                            | `yin/vm/data.cljc`, `yin/vm/values.cljc` (table C blocks stay, as exempt protocol syntax),      | stays for `generate` only.  |\n   741\t|                            | `dao/stream/chunks.cljc`, `dao/stream/base64.cljc`, `dao/jing/dht.cljc`, `yin/vm/linker.cljc`,  |                             |\n   742\t|                            | `dao/stream/datagram.cljc` (bytes arms only).                                                   |                             |\n   743\t+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   744\t| Profile (the arms belong   | `dao/host/{integer,float,number,bytes,text,hash,sign}.cljc` (seven, P), `dao/data/arrays.cljc`. | Stay.                       |\n   745\t| here by design)            |                                                                                                 |                             |\n   746\t+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   747\t| Retained runtime           | `dao/data/btree.cljc` (atomic flag `:102-119`, weak and soft references `:212-245`: substantive | Stay, with the table C      |\n   748\t| prerequisites (table C)    | arms, not protocol syntax, retained by design); `dao/data/btree/storage.cljc` (promise facade,  | reason recorded in the      |\n   749\t|                            | deferred §9.1); `dao/pretty.cljc` (`StringWriter`/pprint, display only, §3.1).                  | gate's list.                |\n   750\t+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   751\t| Codec-track files          | `dao/stream/ws.cljc`, `transit.cljc`, `cbor.cljc`, `cbor/boring.cljc`, `codec.cljc`,            | Stay; owned by that track;  |\n   752\t| (untouched by this track)  | `remote_channel.cljc`, `ws_project.cljc`, `jing/stream.cljc`.                                   | `codec.cljc:45` after C4.   |\n   753\t+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   754\t| Seams and driver           | `src/clj`, `src/cljs`, `src/cljd` seam and shadow files (§3.1 lane-specific list);              | Stay.                       |\n   755\t|                            | `yin/repl/main.cljc`, `yin/repl/host.cljc`, `yin/repl/state.cljc`.                              |                             |\n   756\t+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   757\t| Deferred-scope files (§9)  | `dao/jing/file.cljc`, `dao/space/store/fs.cljc`, `dao/space/store.cljc`,                        | Stay until the deferred     |\n   758\t|                            | `dao/stream/journal/file.cljc`, `dao/jing/content/async.cljc`, `dao/jing/coordinate.cljc`,      | design lands.               |\n   759\t|                            | `dao/space/dht.cljc`, `yin/repl.cljc`, `yang/python/antlr/parser.cljc` and the other            |                             |\n   760\t|                            | `yang/python/antlr/*.cljc` with arms, `dao/stream/udp.cljc`, `dao/stream/journal.cljc`          |                             |\n   761\t|                            | (`locking`), `dao/space/transactor.cljc`, `dao/space/schema.cljc`, `yin/vm/ucf/authority.cljc`  |                             |\n   762\t|                            | (`locking`), and every remaining closure file whose arms are only `catch`/hint/protocol syntax  |                             |\n   763\t|                            | is **not** on list 1 (it is exempt by pattern).                                                 |                             |\n   764\t+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   765\t\n   766\tThe final list 1 after H4 is the baseline minus the first row (except\n   767\t`sign.cljc`), plus the seven profile files. The H2 and H3 library greps\n   768\t(`grep -rl`, recursive) are consistent with it: the libraries they name\n   769\tappear only under `dao/host/` and, for Ed25519, in `sign.cljc`'s `generate`.\n   770\t\n   771\t### 8.1 File-disjointness from the codec track, including tests (re-checked, V)\n   772\t\n   773\tCodec-track source files (`codec.md` §7.1): `dao/stream/ws.cljc`,\n   774\t`transit.cljc`, `cbor.cljc`, `cbor/boring.cljc`, `codec.cljc`,\n   775\t`remote_channel.cljc`, `jing/stream.cljc`, `data.cljc`, the four ws adapters,\n   776\t`yin/repl/{serve,connect}.cljc`. Codec-track test namespaces (`codec.md`\n   777\t§7.2): the 22 requiring Transit, including `ws_test`, `ws-codec-test`,\n   778\t`remote-route-test`, `loopback-net`, `transit-test`, `dao.stream.cbor-test`,\n   779\t`dao.jing.stream-test`, `yin.repl.main-test`.\n   780\t\n   781\tH0-H4 source files: `dao/host/*` (new), `yin/vm/integer/host.cljc`,\n   782\t`dao/jing/cbor.cljc`, `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`, `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd`,\n   783\t`dao/jing.cljc`, `yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc`, `yin/vm/debruijn.cljc`,\n   784\t`yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc`, `yin/vm/data.cljc`, `yin/vm/values.cljc`,\n   785\t`yin/vm.cljc`, `yin/vm/engine.cljc`, `yin/vm/integer.cljc`,\n   786\t`dao/stream/chunks.cljc`, `dao/stream/base64.cljc`, `dao/jing/dht.cljc`,\n   787\t`yin/vm/linker.cljc`, `dao/stream/datagram.cljc`, `dao/data/arrays.cljc`.\n   788\tH0-H4 test files: `dao/host/*_test` (new), `yin/vm/integer_test`,\n   789\t`yang/python/antlr/{int_heap,int_literal,int_ops}_test`, `dao/jing/cbor_*`,\n   790\t`dao/jing_test`, `yin/vm/linker/sign_test`, `dao/stream/base64_test`,\n   791\t`yin/vm/debruijn_code_test`, `yin/vm/data_test`, `dao/data/btree_*`.\n   792\t\n   793\tIntersection: none, with two recorded deferrals: `dao/stream/codec.cljc:45`\n   794\t(`host-bytes?` over `dao.host.bytes`) after C4, and `dao/stream/cbor/boring.cljc`\n   795\t(the transport writer) is **not** touched by H4. The `dao.stream.cbor-test`\n(defn- new-lease\n  []\n  #?(:cljd (EditBox. true)\n     :clj (java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean. true)\n     :cljs (EditBox. true)))\n\n\n(defn- editing?\n  [^Settings sett]\n  (when-some [e (.-edit sett)]\n    #?(:cljd (.-val ^EditBox e)\n       :clj (.get ^java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean e)\n       :cljs (.-val ^EditBox e))))\n\n\n(defn- clear-lease!\n  [^Settings sett]\n  (let [e (.-edit sett)]\n    #?(:cljd (set! (.-val ^EditBox e) false)\n       :clj (.set ^java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean e false)\n       :cljs (set! (.-val ^EditBox e) false))))\n    :test (TestRef. node)\n    :soft #?(:cljd (dc/WeakReference. node)\n             :clj (java.lang.ref.SoftReference. node)\n             :cljs (js/WeakRef. node))\n    :weak #?(:cljd (dc/WeakReference. node)\n             :clj (java.lang.ref.WeakReference. node)\n             :cljs (js/WeakRef. node))))\n\n\n(defn- make-store-ref\n  \"Ref for a just-stored child (§3.1 dirty->stored conversion). When the\n   node's settings carry no storage (a tree built in memory and then\n   stored), a cleared reference could never refault — so pin strong\n   regardless of ref-type; the node was strongly held before the store, so\n   this is not a memory regression. Trees restored through a storage get\n   the configured ref-type.\"\n  [^Settings sett node]\n  (if (settings-storage sett) (make-ref sett node) node))\n\n\n(defn- read-ref\n  \"Deref a child/root slot: nil, a node held strong, or a reference whose\n   target may have been collected (=> nil, refault).\"\n  [x]\n  #?(:cljd (cond (nil? x) nil\n                 (instance? TestRef x) (test-ref-val x)\n                 (dart/is? x dc/WeakReference) (.-target ^dc/WeakReference x)\n                 :else x)\n     :clj (cond (nil? x) nil\n                (instance? TestRef x) (test-ref-val x)\n                (instance? java.lang.ref.Reference x)\n                (.get ^java.lang.ref.Reference x)\n                :else x)\n     :cljs (cond (nil? x) nil\n                 (instance? TestRef x) (test-ref-val x)\n                 (instance? js/WeakRef x) (.deref x)\n                 :else x)))\n\n355:  (str/trimr (pretty/pp-str x)))\ntwo lists (files permitted non-exempt arms, §8.2; resolved effect calls\npermitted) and two negative tests; it scans recursively and treats reading\nzero files as a failure distinct from zero matches, as do the H2 and H3\ngrep gates (status 1 is no match, status 2 is a scan error). Every H1-H4\nacceptance is byte-identical against a named corpus with errata preserved;\nthe three behaviour changes (§10) each have a direct test.\n\n## 7. Consequence for wasm\n\nPrimitives execute in-instance. The import module `dao.stream` has exactly\nthe seven names of `dao.stream.wasm.md:38-46` (V); a primitive import is a\nboundary crossing and a violation of §2.2 whatever its purity. The gate that\nasserts a binary's import list is part of the deferred conformance suite\n(§9.4); until then the rule is stated here and checked by review.\n\n## 8. Slices H0-H4 (co